        "font" : "CMR10",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "Proof. Let {β(n)}∞n=1 be a sequence in S, where β(n) := {bi(n)}∞i=1 ∈ S. Since 0 ≤ b(1n) ≤ a1 holds for any n ∈ N, the sequence {b1(n)}∞n=1 has a convergent subsequence {b1(1n)}∞n=1. Similary, since 0 ≤ b2(1n) ≤ a2, {b2(1n)}∞n=1 also has a convergent subsequence {b2(2n)}∞n=1. Repeating this procedure for each coordinate and applying the diagonal argument, we obtain a subsequence {β(n"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
