        "id" : 7,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 71.998, 253.356, 441.076, 326.23 ],
        "font" : "CMR10",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "n)}∞n=1. For all i ∈ N, the sequence {b(inn)}∞n=1 converges, and 0 ≤ b(inn) ≤ a(i) holds. Deﬁne ci := limn→∞ b(inn). Since 0 ≤ bi(nn) ≤ ai, it follows that |b(inn)−ci| ≤ ai. Moreover, since ∞i=1 ai < ∞, we can apply Lebesgue convergence theorem. This yields limn→∞ ∞i=1 |b(inn) − ci| = 0, which implies that the subsequence {β(n"
      }, {
        "type" : "paragraph",
        "pdfua_tag" : "P",
        "id" : 8,
        "page number" : 1,
        "bounding box" : [ 71.999, 236.55, 441.118, 271.75 ],
        "font" : "CMR10",
        "font size" : 9.963,
        "text color" : "[0.0]",
        "content" : "n)}∞n=1 converges to c with respect to ℓ1 norm. Furthermore, it is straightforward to verify that {ci}∞i=1 ∈ S. Thus, {β(n)}∞n=1 has a subsequence that converges in S. Therefore, S is compact."
